# Maven settings.xml Standards

## When to Apply

When **creating**, **scaffolding**, or **reviewing** Mule projects, or when `settings.xml` is **missing** or **incomplete**, ensure it includes the required Maven configuration for Anypoint Exchange and Mule EE dependencies.

## Required Configuration

The project `settings.xml` (or user `~/.m2/settings.xml`) **must** include:

### Servers

- **anypoint-exchange-v3** — Connected App auth: `~~~Client~~~` / `${connectedapp.client.id}~?~${connectedapp.client.secret}`
- **eu-anypoint-exchange-v3** — Same credentials for EU Exchange
- **mulesoft-release-ee** — Enterprise auth: `${enterprise.username}` / `${enterprise.password}`

### Repositories (profile `deploy-to-exchange`, activeByDefault)

- **mulesoft-release-ee** — `https://repository.mulesoft.org/nexus-ee/content/repositories/releases-ee/`
- **mulesoft-releases** — `https://repository.mulesoft.org/releases/`
- **anypoint-exchange-v3** — `https://maven.anypoint.mulesoft.com/api/v3/maven`
- **eu-anypoint-exchange-v3** — `https://maven.eu1.anypoint.mulesoft.com/api/v3/maven`

## Action

If `settings.xml` is missing or lacks any of the above servers/repositories, add or update it using the template at workspace root: `settings.xml`.

## Reference

- No hardcoded Exchange credentials in POM — use `settings.xml` (see `agents/review/rules/review-pom-maven-standards.mdc`)
- Credentials are provided via environment variables or CI/CD: `connectedapp.client.id`, `connectedapp.client.secret`, `enterprise.username`, `enterprise.password`
